-- The nullary input: an input predicate with NO subject.
--
-- A section `GIVEN p IS A BOOLEAN` (a top-level `ASSUME p IS A BOOLEAN` until
-- 2026-09-06; the two lower identically) is a proposition, not a property of
-- anything. It lowers, and the golden says so: `input …/0 : (goal)`, called as a bare atom
-- with no arguments. That is an ordinary — if unusual — Horn-clause program,
-- and a swipl, ASP or Logical English leg has a perfectly good image for it.
--
-- __The Blawx leg refuses exactly this shape, and the refusal lives THERE.__
-- Blawx's ontology is category-centric: every declaration block hangs off a
-- subject, so a proposition with no subject has no category, no attribute
-- block, no scenario-editor row and no XML image at all — which is the R12
-- blank-row loss its fixpoint harness exists to fail on. This file is the
-- witness that the refusal is one target's block palette and not the shared
-- middle end's fragment: narrowing the middle end to fit it would take the
-- shape away from three legs that can emit it (#258 §2.5 — the middle end
-- records, each leg rejects).
--
-- It also pins the TYPICALLY drop. `days allowed` carries a default; the
-- lowering does not read it, and says so in the fidelity report rather than
-- silently seeding the input. An input predicate is a fact the target asks
-- about, and answering it in advance is not a lossless carry — it is a
-- different program.
--
-- No #EVAL / #ASSERT: the inputs are never supplied (see ./assumed.l4).

§ `Appeals`
    GIVEN `the notice was served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc the notice was served on the recipient
          `days allowed` IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 28

@export whether the recipient is in time to appeal
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is in time` n IF
      `the notice was served`
  AND n AT MOST `days allowed`
